    "b-ks2-path-roster-importers": {
      "id": "b-ks2-path-roster-importers",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The importers — Discord, wiki, PR — run on new exports, mirror syncs, and PRs. They write source rows only, advance their source watermark, and enqueue index tasks. They are deterministic pipelines, not fact writers. The PR importer is a small preprocessing agent: it archives the raw PR and its discussion into the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pr://",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " source and reconstructs the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pull_request",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " ledger row — touched files and targets, outcome, summary — through the same summary prompt the worker summary agent uses. Pure enrichment, so the librarian starts from what the PR touched; it never writes facts."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-ks2-path-roster-summary": {
      "id": "b-ks2-path-roster-summary",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The worker summary agent runs once at run close, from a single prompt. It writes the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "worker_run",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row and its "
        },
        {
          "insert": "submission",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows — the run object: hypothesis, checkpoints and scored tries, outcomes — and enqueues an attempt index task. It never writes facts; it summarizes and proposes, and the librarian decides."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-ks2-path-roster-runloop": {
      "id": "b-ks2-path-roster-runloop",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The run-loop inserts "
        },
        {
          "insert": "event",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "regression",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "note",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ") at integration failures and boundaries and enqueues an event index task. There is no integration-resolver in this model: an integration conflict is a deterministic "
        },
        {
          "insert": "regression",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " insert plus a re-queue for the next epoch, never an agent — the legacy integration-resolver retires with the migration. Report reconciliation runs on a new report revision and writes "
        },
        {
          "insert": "target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "target_status",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "; it is never an LLM."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-ks2-path-roster-extractor": {
      "id": "b-ks2-path-roster-extractor",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The entity extractor is deterministic extraction from the checkout at a revision — the legitimate writer for the four mechanical entity kinds "
        },
        {
          "insert": "translation_unit",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "struct",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "struct_field",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "parameter",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", the same role report reconciliation plays for targets. It runs with reconciliation on a new revision, inserts missing entity rows, and never edits or retires existing ones on its own. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "game_concept",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pattern",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " entities stay curated — librarian-written. Locals and returns remain not entities."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-ks2-path-roster-librarian": {
      "id": "b-ks2-path-roster-librarian",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The librarian role — the event-driven librarian and the target-driven backfill librarian — is the only writer of "
        },
        {
          "insert": "fact",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "evidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "link",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and curated entity rows, and the only role that searches the knowledge sources. The event-driven librarian consumes the index queue: a new object arrives — PR, run, sync batch, drift flag — and it updates the graph from that object. Every write goes through the resolver — grammar, store-row existence, code digests."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-ks2-path-roster-backfill": {
      "id": "b-ks2-path-roster-backfill",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The backfill librarian is the librarian role with a different entry point: target-driven rather than event-driven. Given one subject, it sweeps every knowledge source — all search surfaces — for everything related and builds or completes that subject's record, under the same write gates, the same resolver, and the same only-writer discipline. It runs on the migration's initial build and for on-demand deep passes, consuming a prioritized target list from a deterministic query — targets ranked by available material across the sources, richest first."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
